config BR2_PACKAGE_WAYBEAM
	bool "waybeam"
	depends on BR2_TOOLCHAIN_HAS_THREADS
	# One arm per SoC family whose backend this build can drive. Each arm
	# names the driver packages the backend links against -- on Infinity6E
	# the SigmaStar OSDRV MI libraries, on Hi3516CV610 the vendor MPP
	# libraries and the OpenHisilicon source the daemon compiles against --
	# so the encoder is offered exactly where those exist.
	#
	# The HiSilicon arm also names the family: neither of those two packages
	# restricts itself to hi3516cv6xx, while the makefile picks the cv610
	# backend only for that family and builds the SigmaStar one for every
	# other, so without the family test a board elsewhere in the tree could
	# select this arm and get an encoder for the wrong hardware.
	depends on BR2_PACKAGE_SIGMASTAR_OSDRV_INFINITY6E || \
		(BR2_PACKAGE_HISILICON_OSDRV_HI3516CV6XX && \
		 BR2_PACKAGE_HISILICON_OPENSDK && \
		 BR2_OPENIPC_SOC_FAMILY = "hi3516cv6xx")
	depends on !BR2_PACKAGE_MAJESTIC
	help
	  Standalone H.265 video encoder and streamer (OpenIPC/waybeam),
	  with one backend per SoC family: SigmaStar Infinity6E
	  (SSC338Q/SSC30KQ) and Hisilicon Hi3516CV610 (V5).

	  On Infinity6E it provides a dual encoder backend, sensor unlock and
	  an HTTP configuration API, and it builds the unlocked imx335/imx415
	  sensor modules here, against the kernel this build produced.

	  On Hi3516CV610 the backend compiles against the public
	  OpenHisilicon V5 headers, links the vendor MPP userspace libraries and
	  drives the sensor as a userspace plugin it dlopens by name. IMX335 is
	  that plugin from hisilicon-opensdk (OpenIPC/openhisilicon), opened at
	  /usr/lib/sensors. IMX662 is this package's own build, opened at
	  /usr/lib/cv610: it drives the sensor clock from the mode the backend
	  picked, while the openhisilicon copy assumes a fixed 37.125 MHz and
	  refuses anything above 90 fps. hisilicon-opensdk therefore drops its
	  copy when waybeam is selected, so the image carries one build of each
	  sensor rather than two.

	  No kernel modules are built here: the image's own loader
	  (S70vendor -> load_hisilicon) has brought the MPP module set up before
	  the daemon starts, and the daemon re-initialises the graph around it.
	  That set is the whole vendor one, which is why this arm adds no modules
	  of its own -- measured at 18.7 MiB of the 92 MiB zone with a 1280x720
	  output on a 128 MiB CV610. The vendor MPP shared objects it links are
	  installed here, though: OSDRV stopped shipping them when majestic began
	  linking the SDK from static archives.

	  /etc/waybeam.json is waybeam's own CV610 default: it encodes into
	  the frame-shm ring venc_wfb, which wfb_tx drains. An image that
	  streams over the network retargets outgoing.server there or through
	  the API; with nothing draining the ring the daemon's frame gate
	  throttles the encoder rather than stalling it.

	  The vendor PQ binary library is not distributed (vendor licence), so
	  isp.sensorBin and the iq bin import/export routes stay unavailable
	  on an image built here. `make stage ... CV610_PQ_LIB=<file>` stages
	  a locally obtained one for bench work, never for an image.

	  Infinity6C (SSC378QE) is not offered here even though the
	  encoder has a backend for it: waybeam's I6C backend targets
	  the 2024-06-18 SigmaStar MI libraries and
	  sigmastar-osdrv-infinity6c ships 2022-09-07. The pipeline
	  streams against those, but AE never actuates and the image
	  is black. I6C returns once the two agree.

	  It is an alternative to the Majestic streamer and cannot be
	  built alongside it: both drive the same sensor and encoder
	  hardware, and both rely on the SigmaStar OSDRV MI libraries,
	  which OSDRV installs only when Majestic is not selected.

	  Installs:
	    - /usr/bin/waybeam
	    - /etc/waybeam.json
	    - /etc/init.d/S95waybeam
	    - Infinity6E: /usr/bin/json_cli and unlocked imx335/imx415
	      sensor modules, compiled here against this board's kernel
	    - Hi3516CV610: /usr/lib/cv610/libsns_imx662.so and the vendor MPP
	      shared objects the backend links; the IMX335 plugin comes from
	      hisilicon-opensdk

	  https://github.com/OpenIPC/waybeam
